      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5f6be62b15f9fa5364f1834c|full_name}: It's the same kind of situation, you're paying a cost that will possibly be worse overall to just determine the type of equality/inequality you need to do. Alternatively it's calling .Equals() internally which results in boxing which could also turn out worse because Unity's allocator is bad.\nI also wouldn't recommend using Utilities.IsValid if you know you're dealing with a UnityEngine.Object in U#. It's more of a primitive to simplify graphs.\nAdding ops for common things like this is something that may happen, but it's a less clear-cut improvement and could end up slowing down the interpreter loop overall more than it helps depending on how poor IL2CPP and such is about it. So it needs a fair amount of evaluation and I think there are other areas that are more likely to have a larger impact that would probably be explored first.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-07-19T03:16:02.289Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "If we had a JUMP_IF_ZERO  and JUMP_IF_NULL equivalent of the existing JUMP_IF_FALSE we could save on extern calls for many sanity checks and logic paths.\nI do many == null sanity checks in code for validating whether an object exists or not. With a JUMP_IF_NULL op, code could be compiled such that the Equality extern is no longer needed thus making the udon script run faster.\nSimilarly JUMP_IF_ZERO would be great to have for validating against numerical default values (which are always initialized as zero).",
